<metadata><name>Little Crater Meadows and Lake</name><desc>or-vielbig-2e:166-2: route read from the book's text and sketch map. One trkseg per waypoint pair, in order; segment sources are in the trk desc. Geometry from OSM (ODbL, (c) OpenStreetMap contributors) and USFS EDW (public domain) where marked; 'straight' segments are off-trail connectors, not traced.</desc></metadata>
<wpt lat="45.174662" lon="-121.679190"><ele>1100.7</ele><name>Skyline Sno-Park</name><desc>source: DB trailhead pin th:skyline-sno-park (as in 167-1); confidence: high; pdf_page: 168; book_elev_ft: 3640; quote: From Skyline Sno-Park ski south on Road 42</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.130246" lon="-121.705401"><ele>1081.5</ele><name>Road 42 / Road 58 junction</name><desc>source: OSM node 37537779, east end of 'Forest Road 58' on 'Skyline Road' (NF 42); confidence: high; pdf_page: 168; quote: to a junction with Rd 58 3.8 miles south of the sno-park. Here turn right</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.149103" lon="-121.742849"><ele>998.9</ele><name>Road 58 / Road 230 (Little Crater campground road)</name><desc>source: OSM node 37352833, Forest Road 230 meets Forest Road 58; confidence: high; pdf_page: 168; quote: ski 2.5 miles to a side road that leads west to the nearby meadows and a small lake</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.148312" lon="-121.745542"><ele>989.5</ele><name>End of Road 230 at the campground</name><desc>source: OSM node 6605832016, west end of Forest Road 230; confidence: medium; pdf_page: 168; quote: 300 yards from the campground and end of the road</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.147788" lon="-121.747799"><ele>987.3</ele><name>Little Crater Lake Trail #500, east end</name><desc>source: OSM node 13199928819, east end of Little Crater Lake Trail #500 by the campground parking; confidence: medium; pdf_page: 168; quote: Located 300 yards from the campground</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.148458" lon="-121.750860"><ele>987.4</ele><name>Little Crater Lake</name><desc>source: OSM node 5251038832 on Trail #500 at Little Crater Lake (water way 148025693); confidence: high; pdf_page: 168; quote: Small, deep, crystal-clear Crater Lake, only 100 feet in diameter</desc></wpt>
